Yup, and as I argue in the NUTS paper (one of the later revisions in the section comparing Gibbs RWM & NUTS) Gibbs costs at least O(d^2) per sample if you do any kind of rescaling that costs at least O(d), which is the same order of efficiency as Metropolis. And unless you use a low-rank approximation to the covariance matrix it's O(d^3), since the rescaling costs O(d^2).

