But what if we scale the shape of the covariance matrix (or the relative values of the diagonals, if you want to start with diagonal scaling) based on the sims, while tuning the overall scale (e.g., the determinant of the scaling matrix) at a target acceptance rate (e.g., 0.25 or 0.6)?  I'd this form of scaling would protect against the jumps getting too small, because if the jumps got tiny, the acceptance rate would get huge.  Unless you're stuck in a little corner somewhere, I suppose.  But being stuck in a corner would doom any adaptation, no?
